1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,340
Words in context questions
give you a short passage,

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,940
a blank, and four words.

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:07,700
Sometimes you won't know
one or two of those words.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:08,539
That's fine.

5
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:12,129
You can still get these right,
if you do one thing first:

6
00:00:12,399 --> 00:00:13,399
predict.

7
00:00:13,765 --> 00:00:16,225
These questions belong
to Craft and Structure,

8
00:00:16,245 --> 00:00:18,945
the largest domain in Reading and Writing,

9
00:00:18,965 --> 00:00:21,775
at about twenty-eight
percent of the questions.

10
00:00:22,315 --> 00:00:24,085
And here's the common mistake.

11
00:00:24,395 --> 00:00:29,135
Students read the choices first, then
pick the word that sounds the smartest,

12
00:00:29,155 --> 00:00:31,525
or the only one they recognize.

13
00:00:31,755 --> 00:00:35,445
But the passage already tells
you what the word has to mean.

14
00:00:35,675 --> 00:00:38,005
Your job is to find that.

15
00:00:38,410 --> 00:00:39,980
Here's the core idea.

16
00:00:40,210 --> 00:00:44,260
Every blank has a clue
somewhere in the text.

17
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:48,430
Cover the choices, find the clue,
and put your own word in the blank.

18
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:50,190
It doesn't have to be fancy.

19
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:53,350
Good, bad, short-lived, doubtful.

20
00:00:53,370 --> 00:00:54,780
Plain words work.

21
00:00:55,267 --> 00:00:57,566
Clues come in a few forms.

22
00:00:57,586 --> 00:01:01,647
A restatement, often
after a colon or a comma,

23
00:01:01,667 --> 00:01:03,516
that says the same thing again.

24
00:01:03,947 --> 00:01:08,326
A contrast word like but,
although, or however,

25
00:01:08,346 --> 00:01:12,287
which tells you the blank means
the opposite of something nearby.

26
00:01:12,307 --> 00:01:16,407
Or a cause, with words
like since, because,

27
00:01:16,427 --> 00:01:17,557
or so.

28
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:19,037
Quick example.

29
00:01:19,057 --> 00:01:22,237
The critic's review was
blank: she praised the acting,

30
00:01:22,257 --> 00:01:24,277
the music, and the set design.

31
00:01:24,297 --> 00:01:27,477
The colon tells you the second
half explains the blank.

32
00:01:27,497 --> 00:01:29,436
She praised everything.

33
00:01:29,456 --> 00:01:33,587
So predict: glowing, or very positive.

34
00:01:34,093 --> 00:01:35,673
These show up in two formats.

35
00:01:35,693 --> 00:01:39,623
Most often, a blank, and the question:

36
00:01:39,693 --> 00:01:44,273
which choice completes the text with
the most logical and precise word or

37
00:01:44,293 --> 00:01:45,293
phrase?

38
00:01:45,642 --> 00:01:48,822
Other times, the passage uses a word,

39
00:01:48,842 --> 00:01:52,382
and the question asks:
as used in the text,

40
00:01:52,402 --> 00:01:55,982
what does the word most nearly mean?

41
00:01:56,002 --> 00:01:57,211
Same skill.

42
00:01:57,402 --> 00:02:00,451
You still use the clue,
not the dictionary.

43
00:02:00,926 --> 00:02:04,176
Notice the words logical and precise.

44
00:02:04,366 --> 00:02:07,296
Logical means it fits the clue.

45
00:02:07,326 --> 00:02:11,936
Precise means it fits
exactly, not just roughly.

46
00:02:12,046 --> 00:02:14,305
Two choices can both be close.

47
00:02:14,325 --> 00:02:16,815
Only one will be exact.

48
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:18,237
Let's try one.

49
00:02:18,917 --> 00:02:25,737
For decades, octopuses were
generally described as blank animals,

50
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:29,487
since they seemed to spend
nearly all their time alone.

51
00:02:29,797 --> 00:02:34,017
But at two sites in Jervis Bay,
off the coast of Australia,

52
00:02:34,037 --> 00:02:39,177
researchers have observed gloomy
octopuses living within arm's reach of one

53
00:02:39,197 --> 00:02:41,887
another and interacting regularly.

54
00:02:42,344 --> 00:02:43,634
Pause and try it.

55
00:02:49,404 --> 00:02:53,784
The clue is right after the blank: since
they seemed to spend nearly all their

56
00:02:53,804 --> 00:02:55,454
time alone.

57
00:02:55,804 --> 00:02:58,494
And the but sentence confirms it.

58
00:02:58,684 --> 00:03:01,344
The surprise is that
they live close together.

59
00:03:01,364 --> 00:03:03,574
Predict: loners.

60
00:03:03,723 --> 00:03:06,054
Solitary means living alone.

61
00:03:06,284 --> 00:03:07,974
The answer is B.

62
00:03:08,403 --> 00:03:10,543
Intelligent is the trap.

63
00:03:10,563 --> 00:03:12,783
Octopuses are famous for being smart,

64
00:03:12,803 --> 00:03:14,383
so it feels right.

65
00:03:14,403 --> 00:03:17,782
But nothing in this passage
is about intelligence.

66
00:03:17,802 --> 00:03:21,143
The right word fits the
clue, not just the topic.

67
00:03:21,163 --> 00:03:24,333
Nocturnal and venomous
have no support either.

68
00:03:24,754 --> 00:03:26,684
Next, a contrast clue.

69
00:03:27,234 --> 00:03:30,524
Although Emily Dickinson's
poems are often brief,

70
00:03:30,674 --> 00:03:33,504
they are far from blank.

71
00:03:33,524 --> 00:03:38,304
Their compressed language and unusual
punctuation can require several readings

72
00:03:38,324 --> 00:03:40,334
before their meaning becomes clear.

73
00:03:40,804 --> 00:03:42,094
Pause and try it.

74
00:03:48,865 --> 00:03:50,115
Two signals here.

75
00:03:50,824 --> 00:03:54,645
Although means contrast:
the poems are short,

76
00:03:54,665 --> 00:03:56,165
but they're not easy.

77
00:03:56,185 --> 00:04:01,164
And the colon clause explains it: they
take several readings to understand.

78
00:04:01,184 --> 00:04:03,555
So the poems are hard to understand.

79
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:07,162
Now the twist: far from.

80
00:04:07,633 --> 00:04:09,893
The blank is what the poems are not.

81
00:04:09,913 --> 00:04:11,693
They're not easy to understand.

82
00:04:11,713 --> 00:04:14,652
So predict: easy to understand.

83
00:04:14,672 --> 00:04:16,412
Accessible means exactly that.

84
00:04:16,432 --> 00:04:18,243
The answer is A.

85
00:04:18,718 --> 00:04:20,618
Obscure is the trap.

86
00:04:20,638 --> 00:04:23,978
It means hard to understand,
which matches the passage,

87
00:04:23,998 --> 00:04:26,648
until you remember that far from flips it.

88
00:04:26,957 --> 00:04:32,138
Lengthy isn't a contrast with brief,
and celebrated has no support.

89
00:04:32,158 --> 00:04:36,098
Always check for words like not, far from,

90
00:04:36,118 --> 00:04:37,248
and hardly.

91
00:04:37,704 --> 00:04:39,844
Next tool: charge.

92
00:04:39,864 --> 00:04:44,004
Before you pick a word, decide
if the blank is positive,

93
00:04:44,024 --> 00:04:45,843
negative, or neutral.

94
00:04:45,863 --> 00:04:49,204
Then cross out every choice
with the wrong charge.

95
00:04:49,224 --> 00:04:53,113
You can often do this even
with words you only half know.

96
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:54,580
Try it.

97
00:04:55,539 --> 00:04:59,920
When the artists now known as the
Impressionists held their first independent

98
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:03,190
exhibition in Paris in
eighteen seventy-four,

99
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:09,430
several critics blank the paintings,
publicly mocking their loose brushwork as

100
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:11,540
unfinished and sketchlike.

101
00:05:12,070 --> 00:05:13,360
Pause and try it.

102
00:05:19,131 --> 00:05:22,271
The clue: publicly
mocking their brushwork.

103
00:05:22,291 --> 00:05:23,391
That's negative.

104
00:05:23,411 --> 00:05:25,471
Predict: criticized.

105
00:05:25,491 --> 00:05:27,711
Commended means praised.

106
00:05:27,731 --> 00:05:28,821
Positive.

107
00:05:28,891 --> 00:05:29,711
Out.

108
00:05:29,731 --> 00:05:33,510
Emulated means imitated,
usually out of admiration.

109
00:05:33,530 --> 00:05:34,700
Also out.

110
00:05:35,112 --> 00:05:37,562
That leaves disparaged and overlooked.

111
00:05:37,632 --> 00:05:39,172
Both negative.

112
00:05:39,192 --> 00:05:40,562
Now precision.

113
00:05:40,752 --> 00:05:42,882
Overlooked means ignored.

114
00:05:43,032 --> 00:05:45,972
But these critics didn't
ignore the paintings.

115
00:05:45,992 --> 00:05:48,242
They mocked them in public.

116
00:05:48,312 --> 00:05:51,162
Disparaged means spoke badly of.

117
00:05:51,232 --> 00:05:51,892
That's exact.

118
00:05:51,912 --> 00:05:53,922
The answer is D.

119
00:05:54,398 --> 00:05:56,728
Now a different kind of trap.

120
00:05:56,757 --> 00:06:00,258
Some SAT words are ones you already know,

121
00:06:00,278 --> 00:06:02,727
used in a meaning you don't expect.

122
00:06:03,182 --> 00:06:07,562
Many economists gave the new trade
agreement only qualified praise.

123
00:06:07,582 --> 00:06:12,161
They agreed that it would lower prices
on many imported goods but warned that it

124
00:06:12,181 --> 00:06:15,671
could also lead to job
losses in some industries.

125
00:06:15,982 --> 00:06:21,152
As used in the text, what does the
word qualified most nearly mean?

126
00:06:21,635 --> 00:06:22,925
Pause and try it.

127
00:06:28,695 --> 00:06:32,434
Most people know qualified
as having the right skills,

128
00:06:32,454 --> 00:06:34,185
like a qualified teacher.

129
00:06:34,255 --> 00:06:36,794
That's what choices A and C offer.

130
00:06:36,814 --> 00:06:40,104
But praise can't have
skills or credentials.

131
00:06:40,625 --> 00:06:41,875
Read the clue.

132
00:06:42,225 --> 00:06:45,645
The economists liked
one thing, lower prices,

133
00:06:45,665 --> 00:06:48,115
but warned about another, job losses.

134
00:06:48,145 --> 00:06:50,004
That's praise with conditions.

135
00:06:50,024 --> 00:06:51,764
Praise that's held back.

136
00:06:51,784 --> 00:06:53,805
Predict: partial.

137
00:06:53,825 --> 00:06:55,725
Limited is the match.

138
00:06:55,745 --> 00:06:57,315
The answer is B.

139
00:06:57,780 --> 00:07:00,150
When the obvious meaning doesn't fit,

140
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:01,760
look for a second one.

141
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:05,870
A few common ones: a
qualified claim is limited.

142
00:07:05,899 --> 00:07:08,640
To check something can
mean to hold it back.

143
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:11,040
A novel idea is a new one.

144
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:14,670
And to appreciate can
mean to increase in value.

145
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:20,440
Harder questions often come down to two
choices that mean almost the same thing.

146
00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:23,960
One is a little too strong or too weak.

147
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:25,800
Tone matters too.

148
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:29,760
In an academic passage, a
researcher examines data.

149
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:33,350
She doesn't eyeball it, even
though both mean look at.

150
00:07:33,797 --> 00:07:35,047
Try this one.

151
00:07:35,077 --> 00:07:41,277
A newly discovered fossil blank the
leading hypothesis about when an ancient

152
00:07:41,297 --> 00:07:43,837
species first reached the region.

153
00:07:43,857 --> 00:07:46,796
Because the fossil's
age is still uncertain,

154
00:07:46,816 --> 00:07:49,277
it does not prove the hypothesis wrong,

155
00:07:49,297 --> 00:07:54,397
but early estimates suggest that the
species may have arrived earlier than the

156
00:07:54,417 --> 00:07:56,267
hypothesis proposes.

157
00:07:56,736 --> 00:07:58,026
Pause and try it.

158
00:08:04,796 --> 00:08:07,655
The fossil raises a
problem for the hypothesis,

159
00:08:07,675 --> 00:08:09,296
but it doesn't disprove it.

160
00:08:09,316 --> 00:08:11,656
Predict: causes trouble for.

161
00:08:11,676 --> 00:08:13,816
Confirms goes the wrong direction.

162
00:08:13,836 --> 00:08:15,966
Simplifies makes no sense.

163
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:19,259
Refutes is the near-synonym trap.

164
00:08:19,279 --> 00:08:21,540
Refute means prove wrong.

165
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:26,460
Right direction, but too strong, and the
passage says outright that the fossil

166
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:29,380
does not prove the hypothesis wrong.

167
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:31,260
Complicates is exactly right.

168
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:33,250
The answer is A.

169
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:36,901
Now, what if the right answer
is a word you don't know?

170
00:08:36,921 --> 00:08:42,511
Tibetan Buddhist sand mandalas
are deliberately blank.

171
00:08:42,861 --> 00:08:47,841
Monks may spend days or even weeks
placing millions of grains of colored sand

172
00:08:47,861 --> 00:08:52,001
into an intricate design, but
once the mandala is finished,

173
00:08:52,021 --> 00:08:56,400
they sweep it away and pour the sand
into a body of water to symbolize the

174
00:08:56,420 --> 00:08:58,631
impermanence of all things.

175
00:08:59,137 --> 00:09:00,427
Pause and try it.

176
00:09:07,197 --> 00:09:09,536
The clue: they sweep it
away once it's finished,

177
00:09:09,556 --> 00:09:11,777
to show that nothing is permanent.

178
00:09:11,797 --> 00:09:13,807
Predict: temporary.

179
00:09:14,290 --> 00:09:15,630
Maybe you don't know ephemeral.

180
00:09:15,650 --> 00:09:16,229
That's fine.

181
00:09:16,249 --> 00:09:18,029
Work through the ones you do know.

182
00:09:18,049 --> 00:09:20,070
Durable means long-lasting.

183
00:09:20,090 --> 00:09:21,340
That's the opposite.

184
00:09:21,370 --> 00:09:24,150
Ubiquitous means found everywhere.

185
00:09:24,170 --> 00:09:25,589
Nothing about that here.

186
00:09:25,609 --> 00:09:30,460
Secular means not religious,
but this is a Buddhist ritual.

187
00:09:30,921 --> 00:09:35,741
What's left is ephemeral, and it
does mean lasting only a short time.

188
00:09:35,761 --> 00:09:37,411
The answer is C.

189
00:09:37,761 --> 00:09:40,421
When you've eliminated three
words for real reasons,

190
00:09:40,441 --> 00:09:42,101
trust the one you don't know.

191
00:09:42,121 --> 00:09:46,410
Don't keep a familiar word that
doesn't fit just because it's familiar.

192
00:09:46,828 --> 00:09:49,078
Last one, and it's harder.

193
00:09:49,308 --> 00:09:54,168
Members of the city council had worried
that the weekend street festival would

194
00:09:54,188 --> 00:09:56,397
disrupt downtown traffic.

195
00:09:56,428 --> 00:10:00,328
After organizers agreed to
pay for extra crossing guards,

196
00:10:00,348 --> 00:10:03,488
however, the council voted
to sanction the festival,

197
00:10:03,508 --> 00:10:07,607
giving organizers permission to
close three downtown streets.

198
00:10:07,627 --> 00:10:12,838
As used in the text, what does the
word sanction most nearly mean?

199
00:10:13,324 --> 00:10:14,614
Pause and try it.

200
00:10:21,384 --> 00:10:26,874
Sanction is tricky because it has two
meanings that are almost opposites.

201
00:10:27,064 --> 00:10:31,804
A sanction can be a penalty,
like sanctions on a country.

202
00:10:31,824 --> 00:10:37,074
But to sanction something can also
mean to officially approve it.

203
00:10:37,507 --> 00:10:41,447
The trap is penalize, because the
first sentence sets up a worry.

204
00:10:41,467 --> 00:10:44,087
But read what the council actually did:

205
00:10:44,107 --> 00:10:48,007
it gave organizers permission
to close three streets.

206
00:10:48,027 --> 00:10:49,126
That's approval.

207
00:10:49,146 --> 00:10:51,677
The answer is B, authorize.

208
00:10:52,112 --> 00:10:54,962
So when a word has more than one meaning,

209
00:10:55,031 --> 00:10:57,882
let the sentence choose the meaning.

210
00:10:57,952 --> 00:11:01,001
Not whichever meaning you learned first.

211
00:11:01,413 --> 00:11:02,873
Here's the full method.

212
00:11:02,893 --> 00:11:05,152
One: cover the choices.

213
00:11:05,172 --> 00:11:08,193
Two: find the clue,
whether it's a restatement,

214
00:11:08,213 --> 00:11:10,302
a contrast, or a cause.

215
00:11:10,492 --> 00:11:12,902
Three: predict your own word.

216
00:11:13,349 --> 00:11:16,929
Four: cross out choices
with the wrong charge.

217
00:11:16,949 --> 00:11:20,769
Five: from what's left,
pick the most precise fit.

218
00:11:20,789 --> 00:11:24,319
Not too strong, not too
weak, and the right tone.

219
00:11:24,790 --> 00:11:29,650
And when you're down to a word you know
that almost fits and a word you don't

220
00:11:29,670 --> 00:11:33,970
know, ask whether the familiar
word truly fits the clue.

221
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:37,249
If you can name a real reason it fails,

222
00:11:37,269 --> 00:11:38,960
go with the one you don't know.

223
00:11:39,424 --> 00:11:40,474
Recap.

224
00:11:40,544 --> 00:11:42,524
You don't need to know every word.

225
00:11:42,544 --> 00:11:47,234
You need the clue, a prediction,
and careful elimination.

226
00:11:47,503 --> 00:11:53,114
Check charge, then precision, and watch
for second meanings of common words.

227
00:11:53,531 --> 00:11:57,831
For more practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter for

228
00:11:57,851 --> 00:12:01,421
words in context under
Craft and Structure.

229
00:12:01,879 --> 00:12:06,019
All the questions in this video
were written for this video,

230
00:12:06,039 --> 00:12:08,339
in the style of the real test.

231
00:12:08,359 --> 00:12:10,608
If this helped, subscribe.

232
00:12:11,079 --> 00:12:15,569
Next up, we switch to
math: SAT percentages.
